      "title": "Assistant Women's Wrestling Coach (Part-Time)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition:   Assistant Women's Wrestling Coach (Part-Time)   Essential Functions: In compliance with policies, procedures, and regulations of Alvernia University, the NCAA, and the MAC, directs and organizes all aspects and job responsibilities of an assistant coach as indicated in the Alvernia University Coaches Manual. Responsible for assisting the University's intercollegiate athletics program and establishing policies and procedures that achieve the parallel goals of development of quality, competitive athletics teams and support of the individual educational goals of student-athletes. Assist in the recruitment and retention of academically and athletically qualified student-athletes. Represent and/or carry out the duties of the head coach in his/her absence.  Maintain an active understanding of all University, NCAA and MAC rules and regulations and report any possible violation immediately to the Director of Athletics. Pass the NCAA Division III Recruiting Test. Promote Franciscan ideals and adherence to the Mission of the University.   Additional Responsibilities:   Attend all mandatory meetings, training sessions, etc. Contribute to the overall success of the intercollegiate athletics program by performing all other duties and responsibilities as assigned by the Head Coach and/or Director of Athletics   Position Requirements: Bachelor’s degree. Prior coaching and/or playing experience (preferably at the collegiate level). Knowledge of NCAA Division III rules. CPR Certification required. Commitment to the mission, core values, and goals of Alvernia University Ability to work nights and weekends.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1b9ea04c-142e-4aa7-8d21-45439ca5b76b&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204897980508_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Senior Administrative Assistant, Student Affairs",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nJob Summary: Serve as the Senior Administrative Assistant to the Associate Vice President of Student Affairs/Dean of Students and the Student Affairs division assisting with management of the day-to-day operations  Essential Functions: Mission, Culture & Leadership Model Alvernia’s Franciscan values through compassionate service, ethical leadership, and commitment to fostering a welcoming and respectful community for all. Cultivate a culture that prioritizes transparency, accessibility, and proactive communication. Provide support to the AVP of Student Affairs/Dean of Students.  Provide frontline assistance to internal and external groups, including answering inquiries, responding to requests, scheduling meetings, processing paperwork, and ensuring a professional office environment.  Assist the Associate Vice President with maintaining calendar and scheduling meetings  Process invoices from divisional expenses for payment.  Process expense reports and credit card reconciliations.  Monitor the budgets of all offices within the Student Affairs division and provide reporting.  Track divisional expenses and ensure accuracy of budget records.  Prepare and provide monthly budget reports to the Associate Vice President of Student Affairs/Dean of Students.  Assist with forecasting and monitoring spending against budget allocations.  Oversee deposits to Business Office and Bank  Support needs of Student Affairs division  Triage student and family concerns to appropriate offices  Order and maintain inventory for office supplies for the Student Affairs Division.  Assist with booking rooms and catering as necessary for meetings and events  Assist Student Affairs offices with student and family communication during high volume times such as Connection Days, First Year Housing Assignments, New Student Orientation, Housing Selection and Commencement  Oversee required student paperwork for items such as:  Student Travel  Medical Leave of Absence  Release from Housing Appeals  Conduct Certification Forms  Professional Development, Customer Service & Representation Lead or contribute to internal training and professional development initiatives that enhance compliance, service excellence, communication, and inclusive practices across the organization. Promote a high standard of customer service by modeling responsive, solutions-oriented interactions with students, clients, colleagues, and external partners. Serve as a positive ambassador for the organization by communicating its mission, values, and offerings effectively to internal and external audiences. Represent the university at recruitment and outreach events, including open houses, orientations, and community engagement programs. Other Responsibilities Perform additional duties, as assigned to support departmental and organizational needs. Qualifications/Education: Commitment to the organization’s mission, values, and service-oriented culture. Minimum 3–5 years’ experience as an executive assistant or similar role.  High-level proficiency in Microsoft Word, Microsoft Excel, Microsoft PowerPoint, and other software. Strong analytical skills with the ability to interpret data, evaluate processes, and implement improvements. Exceptional interpersonal, communication, and conflict-resolution abilities. Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and adapt to changing demands. Availability to work evenings, weekends, or travel as required.   Physical Requirements: Regular and reliable attendance is essential for the performance of this role. Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Must be able to lift 15 pounds at times. Employee Benefits: Join a team that values your well-being! We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to support you and your family: Robust Health Coverage:  Choose from three offered medical plans, plus dental and vision. Financial Security:  Enjoy University-paid life/AD&D insurance and long-term disability, with options for short-term disability and supplemental coverage for you and your family (including accident, critical illness, and hospital indemnity plans). Flexible spending accounts are also available. Exceptional Retirement:  Start saving immediately with our 403(b) Retirement Plan . You will get 100% immediate vesting , a University gift contribution, and a generous match up to 5% of your deferrals. Time Off & Flexibility:  Take advantage of 20  paid holidays each year, including 5 floating holidays, and the possibility of flexible or hybrid work options for many roles. Invest in Your Future:  Enjoy tuition remission benefits for yourself and your dependents. On-Campus Wellness:  Benefit-eligible employees on our main campus receive free access to our Medical & Counseling Center. Employee Meal Plan : This plan provides convenient, flexible dining options on campus at a reduced cost.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1b9ea04c-142e-4aa7-8d21-45439ca5b76b&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204897454026_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director of Residence Life",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nJob Summary: The Director of Residence Life provides visionary leadership and strategic direction for the Office of Residence Life (ORL), with responsibility for creating a vibrant, safe, inclusive, and engaging residential environment that supports student success, belonging, personal development, and community. The Director provides leadership for housing operations, residential education and programming, and staff development, while ensuring effective departmental operations and collaboration across the University. The Director advances the University’s mission and strategic priorities by developing a residential experience that contributes meaningfully to student learning, engagement, retention, and overall success.   Essential Functions: Mission, Culture & Leadership Model Alvernia’s Franciscan values through compassionate service, ethical leadership, and commitment to fostering a welcoming and respectful community for all. Provide leadership and strategic direction for the Office of Residence Life (ORL), ensuring departmental programs, services, and operations support the University's mission and student success goals. Establish clear expectations, goals, and performance standards for professional staff and provide ongoing coaching, feedback, and professional development. Directly supervise and evaluate the Assistant Director of Residence Life and Area Coordinators; provide leadership and oversight for the broader Residence Life team including the Graduate Assistant and Resident Assistants Coordinate onboarding, training, and continuing professional development for ORL professional staff.  Develop and oversee the Residence Assistant (RA) performance evaluation process and support Area Coordinators in its effective implementation.  Foster a collaborative, student-centered team culture that promotes accountability, professional growth, and effective communication.   Housing Operations  Provide leadership and oversight for the University's residential housing operations, including housing selection, housing lotteries, new student assignments, occupancy management, and summer housing.  Oversee residence hall marketing and communication strategies related to the housing process.  Coordinate processes for incoming and returning student housing assignments and ensure they are implemented effectively and equitably.  Oversee semester break and other special housing processes.  Oversee the management of custodial, maintenance, access, key management, and other facilities-related residential operations in collaboration with Facilities and Campus Operations.  Attend Facilities and Campus Operations (FACO) meetings and collaborate with appropriate campus partners to address residential facility needs and priorities.  Facilitate and coordinate live-on housing processes for professional Residence Life staff.  Ensure effective use of housing portal and other departmental technology platforms to support housing and Residence Life operations.   Departmental Administration,  Budget and Assessment Develop, propose, and manage the Office of Residence Life budget in alignment with institutional priorities and established budget goals.  Monitor departmental expenditures and operational practices to ensure responsible stewardship of University resources.  Oversee departmental processes and systems to promote efficient, effective, and student-centered operations.  Coordinate departmental data collection, assessment, reporting, and continuous improvement efforts.  Use enrollment, occupancy, student engagement, and other relevant data to inform departmental planning and decision-making.   Student Support, Community Standards and Crisis Response Respond appropriately to student concerns, residential incidents, emergencies, and other situations affecting the health, safety, and well-being of residential students.  Promote a residential environment that supports student safety, well-being, academic success, and community development. Serve as a Community Standards Hearing Officer as needed. Oversee Level 1 on-duty response rotation  Participate in the Level 2 on-duty response rotation and provide coverage during evenings, weekends, breaks, and holidays as needed. Serve as essential personnel during University emergencies, campus closures, severe weather events, pandemics, and other circumstances requiring an on-campus response.    Professional Development , Customer Service & Representation Lead or contribute to internal training and professional development initiatives that enhance compliance, service excellence, communication, and inclusive practices across the organization. Promote a high standard of customer service by modeling responsive, solutions-oriented interactions with students, clients, colleagues, and external partners. Serve as a positive ambassador for the organization by communicating its mission, values, and offerings effectively to internal and external audiences. Represent the university at recruitment and outreach events, including open houses, orientations, and community engagement programs.   Other Responsibilities Perform  additional duties, as assigned to support departmental and organizational needs.   Qualifications/Education: Commitment to the organization’s mission, values, and service-oriented culture. Master’s degree in Higher Education or relevant years of experience required. Minimum of 2 years of supervisory or team-lead experience, demonstrating the ability to lead, motivate, and develop staff. Proficiency in relevant systems, databases, and software applications. Strong analytical skills with the ability to interpret data, evaluate processes, and implement improvements. Exceptional interpersonal, communication, and conflict-resolution abilities. Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and adapt to changing demands. Availability to work evenings, weekends, or travel as  required. Physical Requirements: Regular and reliable attendance is essential for the performance of this role. Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Must be able to lift 15 pounds at times.   Employee Benefits: Join a team that values your well-being! We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to support you and your family: Robust Health Coverage:  Choose from three offered medical plans, plus dental and vision. Financial Security:  Enjoy University-paid life/AD&D insurance and long-term disability, with options for short-term disability and supplemental coverage for you and your family (including accident, critical illness, and hospital indemnity plans). Flexible spending accounts are also available. Exceptional Retirement:  Start saving immediately with our 403(b) Retirement Plan . You will get 100% immediate vesting , a University gift contribution, and a generous match up to 5% of your deferrals. Time Off & Flexibility:  Take advantage of 20  paid holidays each year, including 5 floating holidays, and the possibility of flexible or hybrid work options for many roles. Invest in Your Future:  Enjoy tuition remission benefits for yourself and your dependents. On-Campus Wellness:  Benefit-eligible employees on our main campus receive free access to our Medical & Counseling Center. Employee Meal Plan : This plan provides convenient, flexible dining options on campus at a reduced cost.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1b9ea04c-142e-4aa7-8d21-45439ca5b76b&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204857000910_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Professional Specialist in Accounting",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nFull-Time Faculty in Accounting Alvernia University’s Header School of Business invites applications for a full-time, one-year renewable faculty position in Accounting beginning Spring 2027 or Fall 2027, as negotiated. This is a full-time, benefits-eligible appointment that includes access to University health benefits and other employee benefits in accordance with applicable University plans and policies. The appointment is renewable annually subject to satisfactory performance, institutional need, and applicable University policies. The Header School of Business is experiencing strong enrollment and growth in its academic programs, industry relationships, and community partnerships. We are seeking an experienced accounting professional and educator who wants to bring real-world expertise into the classroom, make a meaningful impact on students, and help shape the continued development of Accounting at Alvernia. This position is primarily focused on teaching, advising, student development, professional engagement, and service. A traditional academic research agenda is not required. We are looking for a colleague who is collaborative, adaptable, and comfortable working in a dynamic environment where priorities and opportunities continue to evolve. At this time we are only considering candidates who are authorized to work in the U.S. and we are not offering sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g., H-1B visa status) either now or in the future. Position Expectations This is an in-person faculty position based at Alvernia University’s Reading campus. Faculty are expected to maintain an active on-campus presence and contribute to the academic, service, and community life of the University. Teaching assignments may include in-person, online, and hybrid courses based on program needs. This is not a remote faculty position. We value colleagues who build strong relationships with students and coworkers and who are interested in being part of the broader life of the School and University. Alvernia hosts student, recruitment, professional, and community events throughout the year, including some evening and weekend opportunities, and we welcome faculty participation when appropriate. Teaching Areas Teaching assignments will be based on program needs and the successful candidate’s areas of expertise. Courses may include introductory and intermediate financial accounting, managerial and cost accounting, advanced accounting, auditing, accounting information systems, forensic accounting, and related undergraduate or graduate accounting and business courses. Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: • Teach undergraduate and graduate accounting courses as assigned and bring current professional accounting knowledge and practice into the classroom. • Advise and mentor students in their academic and professional development, including preparation for internships, certifications, and careers in accounting. • Participate in curriculum development, program assessment, continuous improvement, and the Header School of Business’s ACBSP accreditation and reaffirmation processes. • Contribute to the continued development of Accounting programs and curricula. • Support experiential learning, employer engagement, and relationships with the accounting profession. • Maintain appropriate professional engagement and currency within the accounting field. • Collaborate with faculty, staff, administrators, students, and external partners. • Contribute to School and University service, recruitment, student engagement, and other shared initiatives. Faculty members who choose to engage in scholarship may do so consistent with applicable University policies and opportunities, but scholarship is not a primary expectation of this appointment. Required Qualifications Candidates must have: • A master’s degree in Accounting, an MBA, or a master’s degree in a closely related business field from an accredited institution. • CPA credential and/or substantial professional accounting experience appropriate to the position. • Demonstrated expertise in accounting practice. • Commitment to excellence in teaching, advising, mentoring, and student development. • Ability to contribute to curriculum development, assessment, continuous improvement, and accreditation activities. • Ability to work collaboratively and collegially in a growing and evolving academic environment. • Adaptability, initiative, and a constructive approach to changing priorities and emerging opportunities. • Ability to contribute to the mission and values of Alvernia University. Preferred Qualifications Preference may be given to candidates with: • CPA credential in active or good standing. • Significant professional experience in public accounting, corporate accounting, auditing, taxation, financial reporting, accounting information systems, forensic accounting, or another relevant area. • Previous college-level teaching experience, including experience with in-person, online, or hybrid instruction. • Experience mentoring students or early-career accounting professionals. • Strong connections to the accounting profession and regional employers. • Experience with ACBSP or other business accreditation, assessment, or continuous improvement processes. • Experience developing partnerships with employers, professional organizations, or community organizations. Work Authorization Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and throughout employment. Alvernia University is unable to sponsor or assume sponsorship of an employment visa for this position. Application Materials Applicants should submit: • A cover letter describing their interest in the position and relevant qualifications. • Curriculum vitae or professional résumé. • Statement of teaching philosophy. • A list of three professional references with contact information. A research statement is not required. Applicants are encouraged to use their cover letter to describe how their professional experience would contribute to teaching, student development, and the Accounting program. For full consideration, applicants should submit all required materials by October 15, 2026. Applications received after October 15 will continue to be reviewed, and the search will remain open until the position is filled. About the Header School of Business The Header School of Business is accredited by the Accreditation Council for Business Schools and Programs (ACBSP). The School provides faculty opportunities to contribute directly to program development, student experiences, employer partnerships, and continuous improvement as it continues to grow. Employee Benefits: Join a team that values your well-being! We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to support you and your family: Robust Health Coverage:  Choose from three offered medical plans, plus dental and vision. Financial Security:  Enjoy University-paid life/AD&D insurance and long-term disability, with options for short-term disability and supplemental coverage for you and your family (including accident, critical illness, and hospital indemnity plans). Flexible spending accounts are also available. Exceptional Retirement:  Start saving immediately with our 403(b) Retirement Plan . You will get 100% immediate vesting , a University gift contribution, and a generous match up to 5% of your deferrals. Time Off:  Take advantage of 18 paid holidays each year. Invest in Your Future:  Enjoy tuition remission benefits for yourself and your dependents. On-Campus Wellness:  Benefit-eligible employees on our main campus receive free access to our Medical & Counseling Center. Employee Meal Plan : This plan provides convenient, flexible dining options on campus at a reduced cost. About Alvernia University Alvernia is a forward-looking institution with a bold vision to become a 21st-century modern regional university. This includes expanding enrollment, strengthening business and entrepreneurship programs, increasing international student engagement, and deepening community partnerships. We seek faculty who are thoughtful, creative, and adaptable and who will contribute meaningfully to Alvernia’s entrepreneurial and collaborative academic environment. Commitment to Mission and Values Alvernia University is rooted in the Franciscan tradition and welcomes faculty, staff, and students from a wide range of backgrounds, experiences, perspectives, and beliefs. Applicants of all faith traditions, as well as those with no religious affiliation, are encouraged to apply. All Alvernia employees are expected to contribute to the University’s mission through service, respect, intellectual curiosity, innovation, accountability, and a commitment to the common good. Alvernia University is an equal opportunity employer. Employment decisions are made without regard to religion or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1b9ea04c-142e-4aa7-8d21-45439ca5b76b&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9200368248406_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Women's Golf Coach (Part-Time)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition:   Assistant Women's Golf Coach (Part-Time)   Essential Functions: In compliance with policies, procedures, and regulations of Alvernia University, the NCAA, and the MAC, directs and organizes all aspects and job responsibilities of an assistant coach as indicated in the Alvernia University Coaches Manual. Responsible for assisting the University's intercollegiate athletics program and establishing policies and procedures that achieve the parallel goals of development of quality, competitive athletics teams and support of the individual educational goals of student-athletes. Assist in the recruitment and retention of academically and athletically qualified student-athletes. Represent and/or carry out the duties of the head coach in his/her absence.  Maintain an active understanding of all University, NCAA and MAC rules and regulations and report any possible violation immediately to the Director of Athletics. Pass the NCAA Division III Recruiting Test. Promote Franciscan ideals and adherence to the Mission of the University.   Additional Responsibilities:   Attend all mandatory meetings, training sessions, etc. Contribute to the overall success of the intercollegiate athletics program by performing all other duties and responsibilities as assigned by the Head Coach and/or Director of Athletics   Position Requirements: Bachelor’s degree. Prior coaching and/or playing experience (preferably at the collegiate level). Knowledge of NCAA Division III rules. CPR Certification required. Commitment to the mission, core values, and goals of Alvernia University Ability to work nights and weekends.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1b9ea04c-142e-4aa7-8d21-45439ca5b76b&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204817958862_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Academic Compliance Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nJOB SUMMARY Under the supervision of the Director of Institutional Assessment, Accreditation, and Compliance (DIAAC), the Academic Compliance Manager manages the day-to-day operation of established academic and health compliance processes related to experiential learning, including internships, clinical experiences, and fieldwork. The position serves as a primary point of contact for students, faculty, staff, and external partners regarding compliance requirements and facilitates processes to help students satisfy requirements established by affiliation agreements, institutional policies, and external agencies in a timely and student-centered manner. The Academic Compliance Manager administers affiliation agreement workflows and compliance tracking systems, maintains accurate and audit-ready documentation, and monitors requirements and deadlines across programs and locations. The position identifies compliance gaps, exceptions, and potential risks and escalates matters requiring interpretation, exception, or institutional decision-making to the DIAAC Director. Operating within established institutional policies, procedures, and requirements, the Academic Compliance Manager contributes to institutional risk management through effective compliance operations, documentation, communication, and process administration. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS 1. Affiliation Agreements & Contract Administration Manage the day-to-day workflow for the initiation, processing, renewal, and tracking of affiliation agreements in collaboration with faculty, academic departments, and the DIAAC Director. Prepare and route affiliation agreements for appropriate institutional review, approval, and signature and track agreements through completion. Maintain the university’s master inventory of active affiliation agreements, including renewal dates, expiration dates, agreement status, and other required information. Maintain accurate, complete, secure, and audit-ready agreement records and related documentation to support efficient organization and retrieval. Apply standardized templates, procedures, and workflows to affiliation agreement processing. Refer proposed revisions or non-standard agreement terms to the Director and other appropriate institutional offices for review. Prepare and submit certificates of liability insurance and other required institutional documentation to external partners. Monitor agreement timelines and proactively notify academic departments and other stakeholders of upcoming renewals, expirations, and outstanding requirements. 2. Student Academic & Health Compliance Manage day-to-day processes for monitoring student academic and health compliance requirements associated with internships, clinical experiences, and fieldwork. Serve as a primary point of contact for students regarding compliance requirements, processes, documentation, and deadlines for experiential learning placements. Track student compliance requirements, including clearances, training, health requirements, and other documentation required by the university or external partners. Notify students of outstanding requirements, upcoming deadlines, and actions necessary to achieve or maintain compliance. Work with academic departments and placement coordinators to verify student compliance status and eligibility for internships, clinical experiences, and fieldwork. Facilitate student onboarding into compliance systems and required training modules. Administer health compliance tracking and reporting for incoming, transfer, and international students to support timely enrollment, onboarding, and progression. Review compliance status across students and programs, identify gaps or outstanding requirements, and communicate required actions to appropriate stakeholders. Escalate compliance gaps, exceptions, or circumstances requiring interpretation or institutional decision-making to the Director. 3. Compliance Systems & Data Management Administer the day-to-day operation of the university’s academic and health compliance tracking systems, including Complio or similar platforms. Maintain accurate, complete, secure, and current compliance data and records in accordance with institutional requirements. Monitor system workflows, deadlines, and routine reporting activities to promote timely completion of compliance processes. Generate and distribute routine compliance reports to academic programs, placement coordinators, and institutional leadership as appropriate. Review data and system processes for incomplete records, discrepancies, or compliance gaps and facilitate corrective action with appropriate stakeholders. Troubleshoot routine system issues and work with vendors and internal stakeholders to resolve technical or operational challenges. Maintain working knowledge of relevant institutional systems, including student information systems and reporting tools, to support compliance tracking and reporting. Apply established regulatory, agency, partner, and institutional requirements in daily operational processes. Recommend improvements to compliance workflows, documentation, tracking practices, and systems for consideration by the Director. 4. Communication & Institutional Collaboration Serve as a primary operational liaison among students, faculty, academic departments, placement coordinators, administrative offices, and external partners regarding compliance requirements and processes. Collaborate with academic departments and placement coordinators to promote consistent application of established compliance requirements across programs and locations. Communicate with external clinical, educational, and agency partners regarding documentation, timelines, and established requirements for internships, clinical experiences, and fieldwork. Organize compliance-related activities, including training sessions, informational seminars, student onboarding activities, and clearance events. Provide clear and timely guidance to students, faculty, and staff regarding established compliance procedures, documentation requirements, and deadlines. Develop and maintain routine communications, instructions, process guides, and other resources that promote consistent understanding and application of compliance requirements. Facilitate communication among appropriate stakeholders when documentation concerns, process questions, or compliance issues arise, referring matters requiring institutional interpretation or decision-making to the Director. 5. Compliance Oversight & Risk Support Manage day-to-day compliance operations in accordance with established institutional policies, procedures, external requirements, and privacy and data security standards. Monitor compliance processes across programs and university locations to identify gaps, recurring issues, or operational risks. Compile documentation and provide operational support for internal audits, external reviews, accreditation activities, and other compliance-related requests. Report significant compliance concerns or recurring process issues to the Director for review and resolution. Apply established policies, procedures, and requirements to routine compliance matters and refer exceptions, ambiguous requirements, or matters requiring policy interpretation to the Director. Implement approved process improvements, corrective actions, and procedural changes under the direction of the Director. Deliver compliance-related training and informational sessions for students, faculty, and staff. Participate in professional development and training to maintain current knowledge of regulatory, agency, partner, and institutional requirements related to internships, clinical experiences, and fieldwork. Travel to university locations and other sites as needed to support students, academic programs, and compliance activities. Maintain flexibility in work hours, including occasional evenings and weekends, as needed to support student and program requirements across multiple locations and modalities. ADDITIONAL RESPONSIBILITIES Perform other duties as assigned to support the mission and operational needs of the university. Promote the university’s mission, values, and commitment to student-centered practices in all areas of responsibility. Collaborate with colleagues across academic and administrative units to support effective and consistent institutional operations. QUALIFICATIONS / EDUCATION Bachelor’s degree preferred; an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience may be considered. One to three years of experience in compliance, higher education administration, student services, healthcare administration, project coordination, or a related field. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple processes, deadlines, projects, and priorities with a high degree of accuracy and attention to detail. Strong written and verbal communication skills and the ability to explain requirements clearly and professionally to students, faculty, staff, and external partners. Experience using Microsoft Office Suite, databases, web-based platforms, or information systems to track records, requirements, deadlines, and other operational information. Ability to exercise sound judgment, maintain confidentiality, and appropriately handle sensitive student and institutional information. Ability to interpret and consistently apply established policies, procedures, guidelines, and requirements and recognize circumstances requiring escalation or additional review. Demonstrated ability to work independently within established procedures while collaborating effectively with multiple institutional stakeholders. Strong service orientation and commitment to providing responsive, student-centered support. Experience in higher education, healthcare, clinical education, or a compliance-related environment preferred. Experience with student information systems, compliance tracking platforms, budget management, contract/document management systems, and reporting tools preferred. PHYSICAL AND WORK REQUIREMENTS Ability to perform the essential functions of the position with or without reasonable accommodation. Ability to travel among university locations and to off-campus sites as needed. Ability to work occasional evenings or weekends as required to support student, program, or compliance activities. Employee Benefits: Join a team that values your well-being! We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to support you and your family: Robust Health Coverage:  Choose from three offered medical plans, plus dental and vision. Financial Security:  Enjoy University-paid life/AD&D insurance and long-term disability, with options for short-term disability and supplemental coverage for you and your family (including accident, critical illness, and hospital indemnity plans). Flexible spending accounts are also available. Exceptional Retirement:  Start saving immediately with our 403(b) Retirement Plan . You will get 100% immediate vesting , a University gift contribution, and a generous match up to 5% of your deferrals. Time Off & Flexibility:  Take advantage of 20  paid holidays each year, including 5 floating holidays, and the possibility of flexible or hybrid work options for many roles. Invest in Your Future:  Enjoy tuition remission benefits for yourself and your dependents. On-Campus Wellness:  Benefit-eligible employees on our main campus receive free access to our Medical & Counseling Center. Employee Meal Plan : This plan provides convenient, flexible dining options on campus at a reduced cost.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1b9ea04c-142e-4aa7-8d21-45439ca5b76b&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204817908278_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Adjunct-Civil Engineering Instructor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nCivil Engineering Adjunct Faculty Pool | John R. Post School of Engineering | Reading, Pennsylvania The John R. Post School of Engineering at Alvernia University invites applications to its adjunct faculty pool in Civil Engineering. We seek professionals to teach both lecture and laboratory courses in person in Reading, Pennsylvania. Most engineering courses are taught at the John R. Post Center, 401 Penn Street, Reading, PA, with some activities potentially using other Alvernia Reading locations. Applicants must be available for weekday daytime teaching, primarily between 8:00 a.m. and 3:00 p.m. This daytime availability is an important requirement for these in-person positions. Lecture courses typically meet on multiple days each week, so applicants must be able to commit to a recurring weekday schedule for the full semester. Remote or hybrid teaching is not available for these pools. Hired candidates join the adjunct pool and may receive separate contracts for individual courses as needs arise. Pool appointment does not guarantee a course assignment. Potential teaching areas Surveying and Geomatics Surveying, field methods, measurement, geomatics, and surveying laboratory instruction. Structural Engineering and Construction Materials Statics, strength of materials, structural analysis, structural design, concrete and steel behavior, construction materials, materials testing, and related laboratories. Geotechnical Engineering Soil mechanics, foundations, geotechnical analysis, and geotechnical laboratory instruction. Water Resources and Environmental Engineering Fluid mechanics, hydraulics, hydrology, water resources, introductory environmental engineering, water quality, and related laboratories. Transportation, Construction, and Professional Practice Transportation engineering, construction engineering, infrastructure, project management, FE exam preparation, engineering design, capstone, and civil engineering electives. Professional Engineer licensure, or progress toward licensure, is especially valued for civil engineering assignments. Preferred qualifications We particularly welcome practicing engineers and technical professionals who can bring current industry experience into the classroom. Prior college teaching is not required. Preferred candidates will have at least a master's degree in Civil Engineering, Environmental Engineering, Construction Engineering or a closely related field and approximately five or more years of relevant professional experience. These are preferred qualifications, not absolute minimums; comparable combinations of advanced education, professional licensure, specialized expertise, industry experience, research, and teaching will be considered. Completion of the Fundamentals of Engineering (FE) exam, Engineer in Training (EIT) certification, and/or Professional Engineer (PE) licensure is a plus. Application and employment requirements How to apply All applications and application materials must be submitted through Alvernia University's ADP employment portal for the specific adjunct pool posting. Submit a current resume or curriculum vitae and a brief letter of interest identifying the courses or subject areas you believe you are qualified to teach. Do not email application materials. Email inquiries are welcome, but a formal application through ADP is required for consideration. Work authorization Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and able to accept employment with Alvernia University without current or future employer sponsorship. Questions and inquiries Prospective applicants may contact Dr. Joseph M. Mahoney, Chair of the John R. Post School of Engineering, at joseph.mahoney@alvernia.edu with questions about the School, the adjunct pool, or potential teaching needs. Application materials must be submitted through ADP and should not be emailed. Equal employment opportunity Alvernia University is an equal-opportunity employer committed to Franciscan values. People of all faith backgrounds, as well as people with no faith affiliation, are welcome and encouraged to apply. All employees share responsibility for advancing a culture of care in the performance of their roles while demonstrating service orientation, respect for all, intellectual curiosity, solution focus and innovation, and mutual accountability. Alvernia University strives to be a community supportive of diverse perspectives and identities.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1b9ea04c-142e-4aa7-8d21-45439ca5b76b&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204817899323_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Adjunct-Biomedical Engineering Instructor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nBiomedical Engineering Adjunct Faculty Pool | John R. Post School of Engineering | Reading, Pennsylvania The John R. Post School of Engineering at Alvernia University invites applications to its adjunct faculty pool in Biomedical Engineering. We seek professionals to teach both lecture and laboratory courses in person in Reading, Pennsylvania. Most engineering courses are taught at the John R. Post Center, 401 Penn Street, Reading, PA, with some activities potentially using other Alvernia Reading locations. Applicants must be available for weekday daytime teaching, primarily between 8:00 a.m. and 3:00 p.m. This daytime availability is an important requirement for these in-person positions. Lecture courses typically meet on multiple days each week, so applicants must be able to commit to a recurring weekday schedule for the full semester. Remote or hybrid teaching is not available for these pools. Hired candidates join the adjunct pool and may receive separate contracts for individual courses as needs arise. Pool appointment does not guarantee a course assignment. Potential teaching areas Biomechanics and Human Movement Statics, dynamics, strength of materials, kinesiology, human movement, and related laboratory instruction. Biomedical Instrumentation and Measurement Circuit fundamentals, sensors, physiological measurement, instrumentation, data acquisition, measurement and dynamic response, and related laboratory work. Biofluids and Physiological Systems Fluid mechanics applied to biological systems, anatomy and physiology for engineers, and engineering analysis of physiological systems. Medical Device Design and Regulatory Affairs Medical device design, biomedical regulatory affairs, biomedical engineering design, interdisciplinary design projects, capstone instruction, and upper-level biomedical engineering electives. Biostatistics and Biomedical Data Analysis Biostatistics and quantitative analysis for biomedical engineering applications. Preferred qualifications We particularly welcome practicing engineers and technical professionals who can bring current industry experience into the classroom. Prior college teaching is not required. Preferred candidates will have at least a master's degree in Biomedical Engineering, Bioengineering, Mechanical Engineering, Electrical Engineering or a closely related field and approximately five or more years of relevant professional experience. These are preferred qualifications, not absolute minimums; comparable combinations of advanced education, professional licensure, specialized expertise, industry experience, research, and teaching will be considered. Completion of the Fundamentals of Engineering (FE) exam, Engineer in Training (EIT) certification, and/or Professional Engineer (PE) licensure is a plus. Application and employment requirements How to apply All applications and application materials must be submitted through Alvernia University's ADP employment portal for the specific adjunct pool posting. Submit a current resume or curriculum vitae and a brief letter of interest identifying the courses or subject areas you believe you are qualified to teach. Do not email application materials. Email inquiries are welcome, but a formal application through ADP is required for consideration. Work authorization Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and able to accept employment with Alvernia University without current or future employer sponsorship. Questions and inquiries Prospective applicants may contact Dr. Joseph M. Mahoney, Chair of the John R. Post School of Engineering, at joseph.mahoney@alvernia.edu with questions about the School, the adjunct pool, or potential teaching needs. Application materials must be submitted through ADP and should not be emailed. Equal employment opportunity Alvernia University is an equal-opportunity employer committed to Franciscan values. People of all faith backgrounds, as well as people with no faith affiliation, are welcome and encouraged to apply. All employees share responsibility for advancing a culture of care in the performance of their roles while demonstrating service orientation, respect for all, intellectual curiosity, solution focus and innovation, and mutual accountability. Alvernia University strives to be a community supportive of diverse perspectives and identities.",
